# Local File Source Of Truth

Apply this rule when work involves repo-external local files, OpenClaw workspaces, wrapper scripts, local config files, or other Windows file-system copies that may differ from the git workspace.

- Treat the local Windows file system as the primary source of truth for those tasks.
- Read the actual local file or runtime copy first before trusting the repository copy.
- Use git only to inspect what changed, not to decide which copy is authoritative.
- If a repo file and a local runtime copy differ, prefer syncing the repository from the verified local file unless the user says otherwise.
- When reporting conclusions, clearly distinguish:
  - local runtime copy
  - local system/config file
  - repository file
  - git diff/history
- Do not assume the repository is the only active copy when OpenClaw workspaces, `.openclaw` files, global tools, or wrapper scripts are involved.
